  "expected": "A B-tree index stores keys in sorted order inside a balanced tree, so the database can find rows in a few steps instead of scanning the whole table. Each node is a fixed-size page, typically matching the disk block size, and holds many sorted keys plus pointers to child pages. Since one node can branch to hundreds of children, the tree stays very shallow: three or four levels can index millions of rows.\n\nA search starts at the root. At each node, the database compares the target key with the keys stored there and follows the pointer for the range the target falls into, continuing until it reaches a leaf. Most databases use the B+tree variant, where all entries sit in the leaves and the leaves are linked in key order. That lets range queries and sorted output just walk across neighboring leaves.\n\nWhen an insert overflows a node, the node splits and pushes a separator key up to its parent; deletes may merge nodes. All leaves stay at the same depth, so lookups, inserts and deletes all cost O(log n).",
